1. You Initiate Deployment

You have a few options to provide the secure value:

Option A: Inline (not recommended for production)

az deployment group create \
  --resource-group myRG \
  --template-file main.bicep \
  --parameters mypassword='MySecretP@ssw0rd'

Option B: Prompted (better)

az deployment group create \
  --resource-group myRG \
  --template-file main.bicep
# CLI will prompt: "Please provide string value for 'mypassword' (? for help): "
# Your typing will be hidden

Option C: Parameter file (best practice)

az deployment group create \
  --resource-group myRG \
  --template-file main.bicep \
  --parameters @parameters.json

2. During Deployment

    Azure Resource Manager (ARM) receives the template and parameters
    The @secure() value is encrypted in transit
    ARM passes it to the resource provider (e.g., for creating a SQL server)
    The value is never written to deployment history

3. After Deployment

What you'll see in Azure Portal:

    Deployment history: "mypassword": "*** (secure string) ***"
    Activity logs: Value is masked
    Template view: Shows the parameter exists but not the value

What you WON'T see:

    The actual password anywhere in logs
    The value in any Azure CLI/PowerShell output

Real Example

Let's say you're deploying a SQL server:

@secure()
param sqlAdminPassword string

resource sqlServer 'Microsoft.Sql/servers@2023-05-01-preview' = {
  name: 'myserver'
  location: location
  properties: {
    administratorLogin: 'sqladmin'
    administratorLoginPassword: sqlAdminPassword  // Secure value used here
  }
}

Behind the scenes:

    You provide the password during deployment
    ARM securely passes it to the SQL resource provider
    SQL Server is created with that password
    The password is stored in Azure's secure backend (not in your template history)

Best Practice: Azure Key Vault Integration

For production, you'd typically reference Key Vault instead:

param keyVaultName string
param secretName string

resource keyVault 'Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2023-02-01' existing = {
  name: keyVaultName
}

resource sqlServer 'Microsoft.Sql/servers@2023-05-01-preview' = {
  name: 'myserver'
  location: location
  properties: {
    administratorLogin: 'sqladmin'
    administratorLoginPassword: keyVault.getSecret(secretName)
  }
}

