Secrets/Private Data - Key Vault Integration (Pure Bicep)

For actual secrets (passwords, private keys, connection strings), use Key Vault:
Step 1: Your Bicep Template (main.bicep)

--------------------------------
@secure()
param adminPassword string

param sqlServerName string
param adminUsername string

var location = resourceGroup().location

resource sqlServer 'Microsoft.Sql/servers@2023-05-01-preview' = {
  name: sqlServerName
  location: location
  properties: {
    administratorLogin: adminUsername
    administratorLoginPassword: adminPassword  // Secure parameter
  }
}
-----------------------------------


Step 2: Reference Key Vault in Bicep Parameter File

Option A: Using .bicepparam format

--------------------------------
using './main.bicep'

param sqlServerName = 'myserver'
param adminUsername = 'sqladmin'
param adminPassword = getSecret(
  'subscription-id',
  'myResourceGroup',
  'myKeyVault',
  'sqlAdminPassword'
)
---------------------------------


Advanced: Direct Key Vault Integration in Bicep

You can also reference Key Vault directly in your Bicep template:


main.bicep
----------------------------------------
param keyVaultName string
param sqlPasswordSecretName string
param sqlServerName string
param adminUsername string

var location = resourceGroup().location

// Reference existing Key Vault
resource keyVault 'Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2023-02-01' existing = {
  name: keyVaultName
  scope: resourceGroup()
}

// Use Key Vault secret directly
resource sqlServer 'Microsoft.Sql/servers@2023-05-01-preview' = {
  name: sqlServerName
  location: location
  properties: {
    administratorLogin: adminUsername
    administratorLoginPassword: keyVault.getSecret(sqlPasswordSecretName)
  }
}
---------------------------------------- 

main.bicepparam
--------------------------------------
using './main.bicep'

param keyVaultName = 'myKeyVault'
param sqlPasswordSecretName = 'sqlAdminPassword'
param sqlServerName = 'myserver'
param adminUsername = 'sqladmin'
-------------------------------------------

No secrets in the parameter file! 
Complete Example: VM with SSH + SQL with Key Vault


main.bicep
-------------------------------------------------
// SSH public key - OK to pass directly
@secure()
param sshPublicKey string

// SQL password - from Key Vault
param keyVaultName string
param sqlPasswordSecretName string

param vmName string
param sqlServerName string
param adminUsername string

var location = resourceGroup().location

// Reference Key Vault
resource keyVault 'Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2023-02-01' existing = {
  name: keyVaultName
}

// VM with SSH public key
resource vm 'Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines@2023-03-01' = {
  name: vmName
  location: location
  properties: {
    osProfile: {
      computerName: vmName
      adminUsername: adminUsername
      linuxConfiguration: {
        disablePasswordAuthentication: true
        ssh: {
          publicKeys: [
            {
              path: '/home/${adminUsername}/.ssh/authorized_keys'
              keyData: sshPublicKey  // Public key - safe
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
    // ... other properties
  }
}

// SQL Server with password from Key Vault
resource sqlServer 'Microsoft.Sql/servers@2023-05-01-preview' = {
  name: sqlServerName
  location: location
  properties: {
    administratorLogin: adminUsername
    administratorLoginPassword: keyVault.getSecret(sqlPasswordSecretName)  // Secret from KV
  }
}
------------------------------------------

main.bicepparam
----------------------------------------------------------------
using './main.bicep'

param vmName = 'myLinuxVM'
param sqlServerName = 'mysqlserver'
param adminUsername = 'azureuser'
param keyVaultName = 'myKeyVault'
param sqlPasswordSecretName = 'sqlPassword'

// Public SSH key - safe to include
param sshPublicKey = 'ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABAQC...'
----------------------------------------------------------
Understand:

Type	                     Where to Store	                   Bicep Approach
SSH Public Key	           Parameter file is OK	             @secure() optional but doesn't hurt
Passwords	                 Key Vault	                       Reference with keyVault.getSecret()
API Keys	                 Key Vault	                       Reference with keyVault.getSecret()
Connection Strings	       Key Vault	                       Reference with keyVault.getSecret()
Private Keys	             Key Vault	                       Reference with keyVault.getSecret() 


